* {{homophones|en|mined}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪnd|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The capability for [[rational]] [[thought]].
#: {{ux|en|Despite advancing age, his '''mind''' was still as sharp as ever.}}
#* {{RQ:Whetstone Rocke of Regard|page=291|passage=And ſure, although it was invented to eaſe his '''mynde''' of griefe, there be a number of caveats therein to forewarne other young gentlemen to [[forstand|foreſtand]] with good government their folowing yl fortunes; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|text=“{{...}} it is not fair of you to bring against mankind double weapons&thinsp;! Dangerous enough you are as woman alone, without bringing to your aid those gifts of '''mind''' suited to problems which men have been accustomed to arrogate to themselves.”}}
# The [[ability]] to be [[aware]] of things.
#: {{ux|en|There was no doubt in his '''mind''' that they would win.}}
# The ability to [[remember]] things.
#: {{ux|en|My '''mind''' just went blank.}}
# The ability to [[focus]] the thoughts.
#: {{ux|en|I can’t keep my '''mind''' on what I’m doing.}}
# Somebody that embodies certain mental qualities.
#: {{ux|en|He was one of history’s greatest '''minds'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Ginsberg Howl|chapter={{w|Howl (poem)|Howl}}|page=9|passage=I saw the best '''minds''' of my generation destroyed by madness, starving hysterical naked, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 November 16|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Can Merriman use his rail knowledge to make a difference?|journal=RAIL|issue=970|page=45|text=That's far from the promised land set out in the ''Williams-Shapps Plan for Rail'', that the railways would have a guiding '''mind''' that would be in control of the industry's finances. Businesses have what is called a profit and loss account, showing both revenue and costs, but the current situation means that the two sides of the system are in different hands - and neither is (as yet) in the hands of a 'guiding '''mind''''.}}
# [[judgment|Judgment]], [[opinion]], or [[view]].
#: {{ux|en|He changed his '''mind''' after hearing the speech.}}
# [[desire|Desire]], [[inclination]], or [[intention]].
#: {{ux|en|She had a '''mind''' to go to Paris.}}
#: {{ux|en|I have [[half a mind]] to do it myself.}}
#: {{ux|en|I am of a '''mind''' to listen.}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Complete|Hauke|64|lines=94–99|I fortuned to come in,<br>Thys rebell to behold,<br>Whereof I hym controld;<br>But he sayde that he wolde<br>Agaynst my '''mynde''' and wyll<br>In my church hawke styll.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|author=Charles Dickens|title=A Tale of Two Cities|passage=Although Miss Pross, through her long association with a French family, might have known as much of their language as of her own, if she had had a mind, she had no '''mind''' in that direction {{...}} So her manner of marketing was to plump a noun-substantive at the head of a shopkeeper without any introduction in the nature of an article {{...}}}}
# A healthy mental state.
#: {{ux|en|I, ______ being of sound '''mind''' and body, do hereby{{nb...}}}}
#: {{ux|en|You are losing your '''mind'''.}}
# {{lb|en|philosophy}} The [[non-]][[material]] [[substance]] or set of [[process]]es in which [[consciousness]], [[perception]], [[affectivity]], [[judgement]], [[thinking]], and [[will]] are based.
#: {{ux|en|The nature of the '''mind''' is a major topic in philosophy.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1699|author={{w|Sir William Temple, 1st Baronet|William Temple}}|title=Heads designed for an essay on conversations|url=http://essays.quotidiana.org/temple/heads_for_conversation/
|passage=Study gives strength to the '''mind'''; conversation, grace: the first apt to give stiffness, the other suppleness: one gives substance and form to the statue, the other polishes it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author=Samuel Knaggs|title=Unsoundness of Mind Considered in Relation to the Question of Responsibility for Criminal Acts|page=19
|passage=The '''mind''' is that part of our being which thinks and wills, remembers and reasons; we know nothing of it except from these functions.}}
#* {{RQ:Pyle Robin Hood|chapter=V|passage=Thus they dwelled for nearly a year, and in that time Robin Hood often turned over in his '''mind''' many means of making an even score with the Sheriff.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage={{...}} St. Bede's at this period of its history was perhaps the poorest and most miserable parish in the East End of London. Close-packed, crushed by the buttressed height of the railway viaduct, rendered airless by huge walls of factories, it at once banished lively interest from a stranger's '''mind''' and left only a dull oppression of the spirit.}}
# Continual [[prayer]] on a dead person's behalf for a period after their death.
#: {{ux|en|a month's [or monthly] '''mind'''; a year's '''mind'''}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Attention, consideration or thought.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1849|author=w:Eliza Cook|volume=1|title=Eliza Cook’s Journal,p.119
|passage=They are the “tars” who give '''mind''' to the spreading sail, and their bold courage is the pabulum which will preserve our sea-girt isle in its vernal green to furthest posterity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=Then he, having '''mind''' of Beelzebub, the god of flies, fled without a halt homewards; but, falling in the coo's loan, broke two ribs and a collar bone, the whilk misfortune was much blessed to his soul.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Jolie O'Dell|title=Blogging for Photographers|page=66|passage=If you get a “trolling” comment, delete it, do not respond to it, and move forward immediately without paying any further '''mind'''.}}

