===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To bring or recall to mind; to remember; bear or keep in mind.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1878|author=w:Robert Browning|title=La Saisiaz|line=70
|passage='''Mind''' to-morrow's early meeting!}}
# {{lb|en|now|regional}} To [[remember]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1896''', {{w|A. E. Housman}}, {{w|A Shropshire Lad}}, XXXVII, lines 25-26:
#*: The land where I shall '''mind''' you not / Is the land where all's forgot.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|or|dialectal}} To remind; put one's mind on.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|IV|3|passage=Farewell, kind lord; fight valiantly to-day: / And yet I do thee wrong to '''mind''' thee of it, / For thou art framed of the firm truth of valour.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|III|2|passage=Let me be punished, that have '''minded''' you Of what you should forget.}}
#* '''1684-1690''', {{w|Thomas Burnet}}, ''The Sacred Theory of the Earth''
#*: I desire to '''mind''' those persons of what Saint Austin hath said.
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=This '''mind'''s me of a cobbling colonel of famous memory.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1689|author=w:John Locke|title=w:An Essay Concerning Human Understanding|chapter=Of True and False Ideas
|passage=I shall only '''mind''' him, that the contrary supposition, if it could be proved, is of little use.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History
|passage=He '''minded''' them of the mutability of all earthly things.}}
# To turn one's mind to; to observe; to notice.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|2|passage=Here comes a spirit of his, and to torment me / For bringing wood in slowly. I'll fall flat; / Perchance he will not '''mind''' me.}}
# To regard with attention; to treat as of consequence.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|12|16|passage=Be of the same mind one toward another. '''Mind''' not high things, but condescend to men of low estate. Be not wise in your own conceits.}}
#* '''1907''' {{w|E.M. Forster}}, ''The Longest Journey'', Part I, V [Uniform ed., p. 63]:
#*: It's the worst thing that can ever happen to you in all your life, and you've got to '''mind''' it—you've got to '''mind''' it. They'll come saying, 'Bear up—trust to time.' No, no; they're wrong. '''Mind''' it.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the imperative}} To pay attention or heed to so as to obey; hence to obey; to make sure, to [[take care]] ({{m|en|that}}). {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Mind''' you don't knock that glass over.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|_|except in phrases}} To pay attention to, in the sense of occupying one's mind with, to heed. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|You should '''mind''' your own business.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|I|i|passage=My lord, you nod: you do not '''mind''' the play.}}
#* '''1712''', {{w|Joseph Addison}}, ''Spectator'', No. 383 (May 20, 1710:
#*: Upon my coming down, I found all the Children of the Family got about my old Friend, and my Landlady herself, who is a notable prating Gossip, engaged in a Conference with him; being mightily pleased with his stroaking her little Boy upon the Head, and bidding him be a good Child and '''mind''' his Book.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=w:George R.R. Martin|title=A Storm of Swords|page=84|publisher=Bantam|year_published=2011
|passage=Should you ever have a son, Sansa, beat him frequently so he learns to '''mind''' you.}}
# To [[look after]], to [[take care of]], especially for a short period of time. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Would you '''mind''' my bag for me?}}
# To be [[careful]] about. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Gillie Bolton|title=Reflective Practice: Writing And Professional Development|page=xv|isbn=9781848602120
|passage=Bank Underground Station, London, is built on a curve, leaving a potentially dangerous gap between platform and carriage to trap the unwary. The loudspeaker voice instructs passengers to "'''Mind''' the gap": the boundary between train and platform.}}
# {{lb|en|now|obsolete|except in|dialect}} To purpose, intend, plan.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=IV|scene=i|passage=I '''mind''' to tell him plainly what I think.}}
#* {{RQ:R. F. Burton Arabian Nights|passage={{...}}and if ever I refused to do his bidding or loitered or took my leisure he beat me with his feet more grievously than if I had been beaten with whips. He ceased not to signal with his hand wherever he was '''minded''' to go; so I carried him about the island, like a captive slave, and he bepissed and conskited my shoulders and back, dismounting not night nor day; and whenas he wished to sleep he wound his legs about his neck and leaned back and slept awhile, then arose and beat me; whereupon I sprang up in haste, unable to gainsay him because of the pain he inflicted on me.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland}} Take note; {{n-g|used to point out an exception or caveat.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm not very healthy. I do eat fruit sometimes, '''mind'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|to object}} {{lb|en|originally and chiefly in negative or interrogative constructions}} To [[dislike]], to [[object]] to; to be bothered by. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I wouldn't '''mind''' an ice cream right now.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you '''mind''' if I smoke?}}

